Title: "Type Resolution"
Id: "2b59cf98-6cf3-4b4b-835d-baabd8ee216b"

Text: "A binary operator resolves in this order:

1. **application desugar** — when the symbol names a bound value in scope,
   `x * y` is `*(x, y)` and `x |op| y` is `op(x, y)`;
2. a **colon-qualified capability** — `:op`, `op:`, or `:op:` picks the operand
   type that owns the operator;
3. a matching **`Disambiguates:`** entry;
4. a **capability owned by the operands' common type**, where a value known only
   through a spec (`y \"in\" M`) is first reduced to its `is` facts to make the
   match.

If none apply, the operator is reported unresolved. Prefix and postfix
operators, and member access, follow the analogous capability and disambiguation
paths.

Directed operator forms select the type used for resolution:

```text
x :* y      -- use the type of x
x *: y      -- use the type of y
x :*: y     -- use the least common ancestor type
```

The same idea applies to named operators such as `x :|op| y`.

Capabilities from `Requires:` and `Enables:` are considered together for
notation lookup, and resolution searches the selected type and its parent types
through `extends:`.

Build-backed `from:` capabilities apply only when the value was introduced with
a compatible built literal, such as `A := \set@{x_ : x_ is \real}`."
Id: "b4f93e01-3b86-4228-b3ed-512add4b38bf"

SectionTitle: "Builds And Coercion"
Id: "88aed822-a5b4-4f56-8966-ee206c1c5290"

Text: "A build views a value at a stated type: `\type@value` is a **soft** build and
`\type@!value` is a **hard** build. They replace the removed `value as \type`
and `value as! \type` cast expressions.

A soft build succeeds when the value already has that type, has a parent type
that extends to it, or its type (or a parent) has an `Enables:` `relation:` to
the target marked `represents: \\coercion`. A hard build performs the same checks
and additionally allows relations marked `represents: \\encoding` — an
abstraction boundary that no automatic coercion or soft build crosses.

The named counterparts are `x is \type` (soft view) and `x is! \type` (hard
view).

Coercion relationships are applied when checking whether an already-resolved
command's arguments satisfy its requirements — a command requiring
`x is \rational` may accept an integer argument when `\integer` has a coercion
relation to `\rational`. They are **not** used for operator resolution: `+` on
integers will not resolve to `+` on rationals merely because integers can be
viewed as rationals."
Id: "40a048de-3573-4d73-ad8c-ca8c1772687d"
